# strict-bad-v1 — a DELIBERATELY ill-formed policy (spec §8.1 Path-B violation)
#
# This policy exists solely to witness the R12 well-formedness rejection (example R2). It declares
# a STRICT rule whose conclusion `derived(X)` is also a side of a declared `contrary` pair. Per
# spec §8.1 (Path B), no strict-reachable proposition may appear in a `contrary` pair: a strict
# conclusion is deductively forced, so making it defeasibly contrary to something would let a
# defeasible attack contradict a strict consequence. `checkUnit` stage 2 (Policy.firstViolation)
# rejects any unit carrying this policy with class R12 — before any argument is even checked.
#
# Contrast empirical-v1, whose closing comment certifies the opposite: none of its contrary
# propositions is strict-reachable (it is defeasible-only), so it is accepted.

policy strict-bad-v1

# A strict rule. `allow-trusted` / `certifiers` are the strict-rule surface (grammar §4); they are
# present here only to exercise that the parser accepts a full strict-rule header.
rule s(X)
  mode          = strict
  premises      = [ basis(X) ]
  conclusion    = derived(X)
  allow-trusted = true
  certifiers    = []

# THE VIOLATION: `derived(X)` is the strict rule's conclusion (strict-reachable) AND a side of
# this contrary pair. Path B forbids exactly this overlap.
contrary derived(X) refuted(X)
contrary refuted(X) derived(X)
